            <span class="stat-label"
              >Everyday scenarios mapped to a specific tool</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ SPIN WHEEL ═══ -->
        <h2 id="spin-wheel">1. Spin the Wheel</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-spin">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">🎡</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Spin the Wheel</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                Custom options, animated, built to be watched
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-best">⭐ Most Versatile</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <figure class="tool-body-image">
              <img
                src="/eng-number-picker-wheel.webp"
                alt="Close-up of a bright multi-coloured spin wheel mid-spin with a pointer arrow, names visible on the segments"
                loading="lazy"
                width="640"
                height="360"
              />
            </figure>
            <p>
              A spin wheel lets you type in any list of options — names, chores,
              topics, prizes — and turns it into slices on a wheel that spins to
              a stop on one winner. What sets it apart from every other tool
              here is that the whole process happens where everyone can see it.
              There's no hidden calculation, no black-box output — just a
              visible spin that ends where it ends.
            </p>
            <p>
              That visibility is what makes it the default choice anywhere trust
              matters: a classroom picking a volunteer, a livestream picking a
              giveaway winner, a team deciding who presents first. Repeating an
              entry weights its odds automatically, and a "remove after spin"
              setting prevents the same name coming up twice in a row. For more
              on where it's genuinely useful,
              <a href="/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner" class="ilink"
                >our roundup of practical spin-wheel use cases</a
              >
              covers a wider set of examples than we have room for here.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Accepts up to 500 custom text entries (single or bulk paste)</li>
                  <li>Visible process builds trust with a live audience</li>
                  <li>
                    Handles solo use, small groups, and large crowds equally
                    well
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Repeat entries create weighted odds with no extra settings
                  </li>
                  <li>Reusable — save a wheel and spin it again next week</li>
                  <li>Free, no login, works on phones and desktops alike</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Needs a moment of setup before the first spin</li>
                  <li>
                    Segments get hard to read past a hundred or so entries
                  </li>
                  <li>More tool than you need for a simple yes/no call</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              You're picking in front of people who need to see it happen fairly
              — giveaways, classrooms, team assignments, and party games. See
              our
              <a href="/blogs/how-to-run-online-giveaway" class="ilink"
                >giveaway walkthrough</a
              >
              and
              <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-classroom-activities" class="ilink"
                >classroom guide</a
              >
              for step-by-step setups.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ COIN FLIP ═══ -->
        <h2 id="coin-flip">2. Coin Flip</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-coin">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">🪙</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Coin Flip</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                Two options, zero setup, over in a second
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-limited">Binary Only</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <p>
              The coin flip has been around for so long precisely because it
              does one job perfectly: choosing between two equal options the
              instant you need an answer. There's nothing to configure. You
              flip, you read the result, you move on.
            </p>
            <p>
              Its ceiling shows up the moment a third option enters the picture.
              People sometimes try chaining flips to cover four or eight
              choices, but the resulting probabilities stop being equal and the
              process gets needlessly confusing. Once you're past two options,
              it's time to switch tools.
            </p>
            <p>
              It also carries almost no weight with an audience — a screenshot
              of "heads" proves nothing to anyone who wasn't standing next to
              you. If you want the same instant binary decision but with a
              result an audience can actually watch happen, the
              <a href="/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use" class="ilink"
                >Yes or No wheel</a
              >
              covers the same ground with a visible spin instead of an invisible
              toss.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Truly instant — nothing to set up</li>
                  <li>Clean 50/50 odds for exactly two options</li>
                  <li>Understood by literally everyone</li>
                  <li>Works with an actual physical coin, no device needed</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Falls apart past two options</li>
                  <li>No visual moment for a group to share</li>
                  <li>A reported result is trivial to fake</li>
                  <li>Can't hold custom labels or context</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              It's a private, two-option call with no audience — who goes first,
              which route to take — and both outcomes are genuinely fine with
              you.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pull-quote">
          "I noticed I kept 're-flipping' whenever the coin landed on the side I
          didn't want. That's not indecision — that's the answer showing up
          before the coin even lands."
          <cite>— A reader who wrote in about their decision habits</cite>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ DICE ROLLER ═══ -->
        <h2 id="dice-roller">3. Dice Roller</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-dice">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">🎲</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Dice Roller</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                Built for tabletop games, at home in that world only
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-niche">Gaming Niche</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <p>
              A dice roller returns a number within a fixed range set by the die
              type — d4, d6, d8, d10, d12, d20, and combinations of those.
              Inside tabletop gaming, nothing else feels right; rolling
              initiative or checking a saving throw is baked into how those
              games are played.
            </p>
            <p>
              Outside that world, a dice roller loses most of its appeal fast.
              Every output is a bare number, so using it to pick between named
              options means manually mapping each number to a choice and
              remembering that mapping — extra steps a spin wheel skips entirely
              by letting you type the actual option text.
            </p>
            <p>
              It's also a flat, unremarkable thing to watch. Rolling a d20 and
              seeing "14" pop up isn't a moment anyone's excited to witness.
              Outside of gaming tables, a spin wheel or number generator will
              nearly always serve you better.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>The natural fit for tabletop RPGs and board games</li>
                  <li>Supports every standard die type and combined rolls</li>
                  <li>Trusted and familiar inside gaming communities</li>
                  <li>Fast once you know the numeric mapping</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>
                    Number-only output needs manual translation to options
                  </li>
                  <li>Fixed ranges don't map cleanly to odd option counts</li>
                  <li>Nothing to watch — no shared moment</li>
                  <li>Confusing to anyone outside gaming culture</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              You're at a gaming table and the system is already built around
              dice. Outside that context, there's almost always a better tool.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ NUMBER GEN ═══ -->
        <h2 id="number-gen">4. Random Number Generator</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-num">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">🔢</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Random Number Generator</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                Flexible ranges, clean output, made for data work
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-good">Technical Use</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <p>
              A random number generator produces one or more numbers inside any
              range you set — 1 to 6, or 1 to 6,000, it makes no difference.
              That flexibility is what makes it genuinely useful for technical
              work, sampling, and anything where you need precise numeric output
              rather than a visual result.
            </p>
            <p>
              For pulling a sample of research participants, drawing lottery
              numbers, or generating IDs for a dataset, a number generator is
              usually the right call — the output is exact, loggable, and easy
              to document. Need 300 random sample IDs from a pool of 4,000? It
              does that in one click, no visual fuss required.
            </p>
            <p>
              What it can't offer is public credibility through visibility. A
              number on a screen is just as easy to fabricate as a coin-flip
              screenshot, and there's no process for onlookers to actually
              watch. For a deeper look at how randomness is generated behind the
              scenes,
              <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink"
                >our explainer on whether digital randomness is truly random</a
              >
              covers the same underlying mechanics that power both tools.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Any numeric range, fully adjustable</li>
                  <li>Built for large-scale sampling and research</li>
                  <li>Simple, documentable output</li>
                  <li>No setup beyond entering min and max</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>A number still needs mapping to real options</li>
                  <li>Nothing visual to build audience trust</li>
                  <li>Easy to fake in a screenshot</li>
                  <li>No sense of occasion or anticipation</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              You're doing research sampling, generating IDs, or running a
              numeric draw where precision matters more than presentation.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

