        <!-- ═══ LIST PICKER ═══ -->
        <h2 id="list-picker">5. Random Name / List Picker</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-list">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">📋</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Random Name / List Picker</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                A text box that shuffles — fast, plain, private
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-good">Good Alternative</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <p>
              A list picker takes a pasted block of names, shuffles it, and
              highlights or surfaces one result. There's no animation — just a
              text box in, a name out. For private, solo use where you just need
              a quick answer, that plainness is a feature, not a flaw.
            </p>
            <p>
              It stops being the right choice the moment someone else is
              watching. A name lighting up in a text field carries none of the
              drama or shared anticipation of a spinning wheel, which matters a
              lot if you're a teacher, a streamer, or an event host picking a
              winner in front of a group. Our piece on
              <a href="/blogs/name-picker-wheel-work-school" class="ilink"
                >creative ways to use a name-picker wheel</a
              >
              shows how the visual format changes the whole experience.
            </p>
            <p>
              Worth noting: both tools typically draw from the same underlying
              random-selection logic, so fairness isn't the differentiator here.
              The choice comes down entirely to context — private and quick, or
              public and worth watching.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Paste-and-click simplicity</li>
                  <li>
                    Handles very large lists where wheel slices would shrink to
                    nothing
                  </li>
                  <li>Some versions export or log the result</li>
                  <li>Low-distraction for purely private use</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>No animation, no audience engagement</li>
                  <li>A text highlight is easy to screenshot and fake</li>
                  <li>Zero suspense for a group watching</li>
                  <li>Feels flat for classroom or team settings</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              You need a private, fast draw from a genuinely large list —
              500-plus names, say — where a wheel's slices would be unreadably
              thin anyway.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ YES/NO WHEEL ═══ -->
        <h2 id="yes-no">6. Yes or No Wheel</h2>

        <div class="tool-card tc-yesno">
          <div class="tool-header">
            <div class="tool-icon-wrap">✅</div>
            <div class="tool-header-text">
              <div class="tool-name">Yes or No Wheel</div>
              <div class="tool-tagline">
                A visible coin flip, and a mirror for what you actually want
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict-badge vb-niche">Binary Decisions</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tool-body">
            <p>
              The Yes or No wheel is a spin wheel pre-loaded with just two
              outcomes. It keeps the same 50/50 odds as a coin flip but adds the
              visible spin, which makes it a genuine upgrade whenever you have a
              screen handy and want the result to feel a little more deliberate.
            </p>
            <p>
              Where it gets interesting is the reaction it produces. Spin it on
              a question you're genuinely torn about, and your gut response to
              the result — relief, disappointment, a flicker of "wait, no" —
              often tells you what you wanted before you consciously worked it
              out. Our article on
              <a href="/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making" class="ilink"
                >the psychology behind random decision-making</a
              >
              digs into why this "emotional tell" shows up so reliably.
            </p>
            <p>
              It's a solid low-stakes participation tool for classrooms — quick
              yes/no rounds with no lingering — and for everyday personal calls
              like "should I go for a run today?" See our full breakdown:
              <a href="/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use" class="ilink"
                >the Yes or No wheel, and when it's actually worth using</a
              >.
            </p>
            <div class="pros-cons">
              <div class="pc-col pc-pros">
                <h3>Where it wins</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Zero setup — nothing to type in</li>
                  <li>Visibly more engaging than a coin toss</li>
                  <li>Surfaces your real preference through your reaction</li>
                  <li>Great for quick classroom participation rounds</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="pc-col pc-cons">
                <h3>Where it doesn't</h3>
                <ul class="pc-list">
                  <li>Strictly binary — no room for a third option</li>
                  <li>Less spectacle than a full custom wheel</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="best-for">
              <strong>🎯 Reach for it when</strong>
              Any genuine yes/no call where you'd like a bit more ceremony — or
              honesty — than a coin flip gives you.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

