        <h2 id="faq">Common Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Is one of these tools actually more random than the others?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Not meaningfully when well implemented—though the math behind the scenes matters. Casual widgets often use basic <code>Math.random()</code>, whereas Spin The Wheels Online draws from browser Web Cryptography (<code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) with rejection sampling across its wheels, coin flips, dice rollers, and number pickers so no slice receives modulo skew. What actually differs
            between tools is the interface, slice capacity (up to 500 entries on a wheel), and
            the experience of watching the result land. If you want the
            technical detail on how that randomness is produced,
            <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink-teal"
              >our explainer on digital randomness</a
            >
            covers it in plain language.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Could I just use a spin wheel instead of dice for a board game?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            You could — add "1" through "6" as options and spin it like a d6 —
            but it's slower to set up and loses the tactile feel players
            actually enjoy at a tabletop game. For gaming, a dedicated dice
            roller is almost always the better fit. A spin wheel's real strength
            is custom text options and visible audience engagement, neither of
            which adds much once you're just after a plain numeric roll.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Which tool gives a school raffle the most credible result?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            A spin wheel loaded with every entry, spun live in front of parents
            and students, ideally with the moment recorded. That's a lot more
            convincing than a number-generator screenshot or a list-picker
            highlight, both of which are trivial to fabricate after the fact.
            The visible, real-time spin is what actually earns audience trust.
            Our
            <a href="/blogs/school-raffle-spin-wheel" class="ilink-teal"
              >school raffle guide</a
            >
            walks through the setup in detail.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Is the Yes/No wheel just a gimmick, or does it actually help?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            It has genuine practical value beyond novelty — specifically through
            what people describe as an "emotional tell." When you're genuinely
            torn on a two-way decision and spin, your immediate reaction to the
            result — relief, a sinking feeling, an instinctive "wait, no" —
            often surfaces the preference you already had but hadn't admitted to
            yourself. Our
            <a href="/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use" class="ilink-teal"
              >guide to the Yes/No wheel</a
            >
            walks through exactly when this is worth using.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Should I just use an app that bundles all of these tools
            together?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Some apps do bundle a spin wheel, coin flip, dice roller, and number
            generator into one interface, which can be convenient. The usual
            trade-off is a busier interface and sometimes a paywall on the more
            useful features. For most everyday needs, a dedicated free spin
            wheel handles the common cases cleanly, and a quick search covers
            the occasional coin flip or dice roll. A bundled app is worth it
            mainly if you genuinely switch between tool types often, in the same
            workflow.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="verdict">Where This Leaves Us</h2>
        <p>
          Working through all six tools honestly, the picture holds up without
          needing to be oversold:
          <strong
            >the spin wheel covers the widest range of everyday
            situations</strong
          >, but it was never meant to replace the other five — and it doesn't
          pretend to.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're at a gaming table, keep the dice. If you're sampling from a
          dataset of thousands, keep the number generator. If you just need a
          quick, private yes or no, a coin or the Yes/No wheel will do it faster
          than anything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          But for the situations that come up most — choosing from a custom
          list, making a call a group needs to trust, running a giveaway,
          engaging a classroom, splitting up tasks fairly, or just adding a bit
          of shared suspense to an otherwise dull decision — the spin wheel is
          the tool that turns "random" into something worth actually watching.
        </p>
